## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates the ability to design governance that aligns regulatory requirements with product velocity in a distributed, multi-region setup.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-domain decision rights and escalation paths
- SLOs/SLIs, error budgets, runbooks, postmortems
- Phased releases: canary, shadow traffic, regional residency checks
- Rollback strategies and incident playbooks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-gating check for progress
function canProceed(region, changes) {
  if (!regsVerified(region)) return false;
  if (latencyImpact(changes) > SLO.latency) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate regulatory verification across regions?
- How would you handle conflicting gate criteria between regions?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to triage, harden reliability under pressure, and coordinate cross-team remediation with business impact.

## Key Concepts

- Incident containment and rollback
- Safe fallbacks, circuit breakers, backoff strategies
- Cross-team governance and ownership
- Metrics: latency, payout SLA, MTTR, post-incident learning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: simple circuit breaker
class Breaker {
  constructor(threshold) {
    this.failures = 0;
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.open = false;
  }
  call(fn) {
    if (this.open) throw new Error('Breaker is open');
    return fn().catch(() => {
      this.failures++;
      if (this.failures > this.threshold) this.open = true;
      throw;
    });
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure success after the fix? 
- What would you include in the post-incident report for audit and compliance?
